That rule is posted as "[PATCH] claude/skills/freebsd-port: Justify
every Makefile line" [1]. It adds it to the "Write the port" step of
SKILL.md and to spec.org. Before a variable stays in the Makefile, the
port author must know what reads it and what breaks without it. If
nothing does, the line goes; when its purpose is not obvious, the
patch notes say why it is there. The patch applies to b/dev@upstream
and touches lines apart from those that "[PATCH 0/2] Build ports
without building their dependencies" [2] changes.

v2 will go through the port's Makefile line by line under that rule,
not only GIT_HASH. The lines I will justify or drop are the "-s -w"
ldflags, the SIGNOZ_* indirection, SUB_LIST and PLIST_SUB repeating
the same three values, the second WWW, and the .map exclusion.

v2 is not ready yet, because the build failure I reported is not
solved. signoz v0.145.0 vendors github.com/bytedance/sonic v1.14.1,
which builds only on Go 1.24 and 1.25, while the tree offers Go 1.26
and 1.27. sonic v1.15.4 builds through Go 1.27 (its sonic.go is
tagged "!go1.28"), so the fix is to bump that module. That is not a
one-line go.mod patch, though: with GO_MODULE set, Mk/Uses/go.mk
vendors the modules during extract, before any patch applies, so a
patched go.mod alone changes nothing.

sysutils/google-guest-agent bumps modules the other way. It does not
set GO_MODULE; it lists every module in GH_TUPLE, as "make
gomod-vendor" generates them, and patches go.mod to the versions it
fetches. For signoz that would mean a long GH_TUPLE list in place of
GO_MODULE. Before choosing, I will find how other GO_MODULE ports get
past a vendored module too old for the tree's Go, and check whether
sonic's own dependencies (sonic/loader and others) must move with it.
v2 goes out only once stage and check-plist pass.

